Subject: Re: Observability hooks and SDK integration - post-demo follow-up

Hi Alex - Marcus on infra here. Quick answers and a small snippet you can drop into your Node API gateway.

1) Trace IDs: we can accept AWS X-Ray trace IDs. If you pass X-Amzn-Trace-Id we'll map it to X-Redwood-Trace-Id on ingress. If you prefer to keep X-Redwood-Trace-Id we can accept that too.

2) Streaming token correlation: X-Redwood-Request-Id will correlate logs and metrics for the whole API call. For per-chunk timing we recommend optionally including a sequence number in the JSON stream (e.g., {"seq": 1, "token_time_ms": 12}). We also emit server-side token timing in structured logs when streaming mode is used.

3) OAuth flow: we support short-lived client credentials tokens via our OAuth endpoint (expires in 15 minutes by default, refresh using the client_secret). We can extend TTL for Dedicated/Private if you need longer windows.

Snippet (Node middleware):

const express = require('express');
const app = express();

function redwoodHeaders(req, res, next) {
  // propagate or normalize trace id
  const xray = req.headers['x-amzn-trace-id'];
  const traceId = xray || req.headers['x-redwood-trace-id'] || generateUUID();
  req.headers['x-redwood-trace-id'] = traceId;
  // request id for per-call metrics
  req.headers['x-redwood-request-id'] = req.headers['x-redwood-request-id'] || generateUUID();
  // attach routing hint if present from upstream
  if (req.headers['x-routing-hint']) {
    req.headers['x-redwood-routing'] = req.headers['x-routing-hint'];
  }
  next();
}

app.use(redwoodHeaders);

// helper: generateUUID() - use standard lib or existing util

